WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:05.180
At the 2000 Republican National
Convention

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.960
the country got one of its first glimpses of a new type of public charter school.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:17.140
The slogans of our schools are not, "all of us can learn" but rather "all of us will learn."

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.040
The claim was that with enough rigor and devotion

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:26.020
such schools could close the achievement gap between poor minorities and their wealthy white counterparts.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:33.700
There are no shortcuts. You're here at 7:25, you stay until 5:00, you suffer through two hours of homework,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.960
you come on Saturdays, and you come during the summer.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.000
The shining example was the Knowledge is Power Program or KIPP.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.800
It's a public school unlike just about any
you have seen before.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.800
There's no more excuses, the school down the block can no longer say well what can we do?

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:55.300
But skeptics pointed out that the families showing up at KIPP and other "no excuses" charters were self-selected.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:00.160
Charter schools by definition are not random collections of students.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.600
Students who choose to go to charter schools are likely to have more motivated parents.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:10.880
Every student, every parent, and every teacher must commit to KIPP's regimen of long hours and hard work—

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.540
—in writing.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:18.600
In 2006 a combative former New York City
Council member named Eva Moskowitz

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:25.460
co-founded a new charter school network with the same approach. I think parents deserve in real time

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:30.960
something better. Success Academy was KIPP on steroids. Trouncing many public schools

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.740
and wealthy neighborhoods on the annual state exams.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.340
Enter the education writer and former public school teacher Robert Pondiscio, who spent a year embedded

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:46.000
at a Success Academy in an effort to figure out just how these schools do it. In his new book,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.840
"How the Other Half Learns," Pondiscio reports that the critics were right.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.658
Not only is the very act of applying to the lottery self-selecting,

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:57.260
but Success Academy makes such rigorous demands on parents

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:01.700
that it disproportionately retains the most highly motivated families.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:06.800
And that dissatisfaction brought as many as 5,000 parents to the armory in Washington Heights.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.260
The result is that an applicant's chances of winning a seat at success in its annual high-stakes lottery

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:18.160
aren't as competitive as many had claimed. Pondiscio found that there are about six applicants for every spot.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.540
However because so many families drop out, the chances of getting offered a spot

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:29.720
are actually closer to 50%. But for those that make the commitment the impact is transformative.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:35.360
And he argues that these kids deserve the same access to a great school that upper middle class parents

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.200
finagle for their children, even if it means leaving the rest of their communities behind.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.940
Let's be brutally honest, when you take that child away you're damaging the public school system.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.880
But where do we get the right to treat anybody's child as a public resource?

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:55.800
I sat down with Pondiscio to discuss why he believes motivated families deserve the opportunity

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:01.980
to exit their district public schools, which a New York Times reviewer called a "morally disturbing conclusion

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.280
to his unsparingly honest book,"

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:12.380
and his challenge to both supporters and detractors of the school reform movement.

